nce  and the question is what happens next  and i should say you know both of those  lines are growing so you've got growth  of about 4.7 when it comes to uh cases  uh 3.4  when it comes to hospitalizations  doesn't sound like much difference but  when i just extrapolate  those growth rates on the next few weeks  here's what happens cases  get up to about 40 000 by july the 19th  that's about two-thirds of where they  were in the winter peak you see that  line's very high isn't  it but if you do the same thing just  extrapolating recent growth rates  on hospitalizations you're up to about  560  uh by july the 19th now that's about one  in ten about kind of a tenth just over a  tenth of where we were  uh in the peak and communicating that's  going to be really difficult isn't it  because  what's july the 19th that's freedom day  and on freedom day we could have  two-thirds of the level of cases that we  had at the peak in winter that's going  to make everyone very nervous indeed  so the question is whether those two  lines you know whether that link has  been broken and whether those trends  continue but right now the trends are  suggesting  quite a big divergence and that's  encouraging because you'll probably  remember charts like this that we saw  around the time of the prime minister  announcing those dates well data not  dates he said  uh for reopening this was potential  scenarios from imperial when it came to  the next few months this is  hospitalizations and you can see those  those lines and those banded areas  they're just kind of confidence areas  you can see that's where they thought we  might head with hospitalizations  what has happened in the actual kind of  eventuality we'll have a look at those  dots on the far left hand side and i'm  going to show you what actually happened  in the following months  just look at those those dots so we are  really low  in comparison with a lot of those  scenarios and bear in mind none of those  scenarios expected the